<h2>The question</h2>

<p><a href="/backlog/4029-a-tooling-caused-delegation-miss-re-graduates-on-the-normal/">#4029</a> asks
whether a delegation-trial miss traced to <em>tooling or instructions</em> — not the vendor's own judgment —
should re-graduate at the ordinary cold-start bar once the fix is proven, instead of always paying the
higher post-miss bar
<a href="/docs/agent/platform-decisions/#delegation-trial-record-graduation">#delegation-trial-record-graduation</a>
rule 5 sets today (<code>minCleanStreak + k</code>, built by #3889). Rule 5 currently applies that higher
bar to every confirmed miss with no distinction for what caused it. This survey does not repeat the #3690
survey (<a href="/research/delegation-graduation-and-supervision-tiers/">When has a delegated non-Claude
agent earned lighter supervision?</a>) or #3673's calibration-veto-clearing grounding; it asks three new
questions those did not cover. The full working is in
<a href="/reports/2026-09-24-delegation-post-miss-root-cause-classification.md">the session report</a>.</p>

<h2>Finding 1 — reinstatement pairs a named fix with fresh evidence against the trigger, never volume alone</h2>

<p>Google's flaky-test quarantine removes an unstable test from the blocking lane, but exit requires an
identified root-cause fix, not just a quiet run: a test returns to the blocking lane once its root cause is
fixed <em>and</em> it has held a run of consecutive passes (Google Testing Blog, "Flaky Tests at Google and
How We Mitigate Them", 2016; current practice guides corroborate the same shape). Quarantine is explicitly
temporary and SLA-bound — a mitigation, never a substitute for the fix. This is the direct analog for #4029
Forks 3 and 4: the fix and the evidence are both required, and the evidence must be fresh against the
trigger, not an unrelated clean streak.</p>

<h2>Finding 2 — classification targets systems, and is reviewed by someone other than the party under review</h2>

<p>Google's SRE book and the surrounding blameless-postmortem literature treat "human error" (or, by direct
analogy, "vendor error") as a red flag that stops root-cause analysis too soon, not a valid terminal answer
— practice pushes past the first plausible answer (e.g. via "5 Whys") to the systemic condition that allowed
it, and tracks corrective actions separately by whether they change a system/process or merely "retrain a
person." The postmortem is reviewed by people other than the party whose action produced it, never
self-certified by that party.</p>

<h2>Finding 3 — ISO 9001-lineage CAPA / SCAR: root cause is investigated, then reviewed by the requesting org</h2>

<p>A Supplier Corrective Action Request requires the supplier to run a structured root-cause method (5 Whys,
8D, fishbone) and submit it back to the <em>requesting</em> organization for review. Practitioner guidance is
explicit that reviewers should push back on "operator error" as a terminal root cause and demand the
underlying process gap (Wikipedia, "Corrective and preventive action"; SCAR glossaries from ComplianceQuest,
MasterControl and Arena, 2026). The classifying party is never only the party under review — this directly
grounds #4029 Fork 2's requirement that a tooling-vs-vendor classification be a human-authored field, never
inferred from free text and never self-declared by the dispatch automation or the delegated vendor's own
session.</p>

<h2>Finding 4 — no surveyed discipline transfers one unit's proof to another unit's record</h2>

<p>Flaky-test quarantine, SCAR, and postmortem corrective actions are all scoped to the failing unit (the
specific test, the specific supplier's line, the specific finding). A shared root cause — the same library
used by ten suppliers, the same infrastructure flake affecting many tests — still requires each unit's own
corrective-action record to close, even when the same root-cause finding is cited across all of them. This
grounds #4029 Fork 5's default: a <code>rootCause</code> note may be cited across every affected triple's
row (the diagnosis is reusable text), but each triple accumulates its own post-fix trial evidence — matching
the already-ratified "trust never carries across triples" (top of
<a href="/docs/agent/platform-decisions/#delegation-trial-record-graduation">#delegation-trial-record-graduation</a>).</p>

<h2>What this survey does not settle</h2>

<p>None of the three disciplines surveyed map a numeric bar onto "proven" — they say "the fix, then fresh
evidence of typical scope," not "N clean trials." This survey therefore supports the ordinary cold-start bar
as the tooling-proven re-entry default, not a fixed lower number; any lower number is deferred to a future
ordinary batched finding exactly as rule 3 already requires for <code>minCleanStreak</code>/<code>k</code>
themselves. The call, the forks, and the ratification live on
<a href="/backlog/4029-a-tooling-caused-delegation-miss-re-graduates-on-the-normal/">#4029</a> itself, not
here.</p>
